# Testing and quality

`docs/TEST_GATES.md` and repository scripts define the official verification requirements. Do not invent replacement quality gates.

## Automated quality gate

Windows: `./scripts/check.ps1`  
Linux / macOS: `./scripts/check.sh`

Those scripts run, in order, failing on the first error:

```text
cargo fmt --all -- --check
cargo check --workspace
cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings
cargo test --workspace
```

Clippy warnings are denied. Never claim a task is complete merely because the code compiles.

Extended network soaks are `#[ignore]` and are **not** part of the default gate. Run them only when the task or `docs/TEST_GATES.md` requires them (`./scripts/network_soak.ps1` / `./scripts/network_soak.sh`). Localhost soaks are not player-capacity claims.

## Verification kinds

Separate:

- **Automated verification** — quality-gate commands actually executed and their results.
- **Manual / runtime verification still required** — visual, multiplayer, timing, networking, or other observed behavior that tests do not prove.

Passing automated tests does not prove visual, multiplayer, timing, networking, or runtime behavior when manual observation is part of the acceptance criteria.

## Test integrity

Never weaken tests, remove legitimate tests, skip failing tests, or change assertions merely to obtain green output, unless the requested feature intentionally changes the underlying contract.

When a test fails, determine whether:

- the implementation is wrong,
- the test exposes a regression,
- or the intended contract legitimately changed.

Protocol golden vectors in `crates/protocol/tests/wire_golden.rs` freeze historical wire bytes. A failure means the wire format moved. Do not regenerate fixtures to make a test pass. Review compatibility, update `PROTOCOL_VERSION` if the change is incompatible, then update affected vectors deliberately (`docs/PROTOCOL.md`).

Report which commands were actually executed and their results.
